{"parentUuid":"3d31a617-26bb-4149-a28c-a2be85b4871b","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6f37bbaf-3b2a-4889-9da8-014105b65b82","agentId":"a13cf5eff78c9773b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CJ5aJ13sdSpywaWC2wcd7x","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Scallop neurosymbolic differentiable Datalog OR-Tools constraint programming\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"[2304.04812] Scallop: A Language for Neurosymbolic Programming\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.04812\"},{\"title\":\"Scallop: A Language for Neurosymbolic Programming | Proceedings of the ACM on Programming Languages\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3591280\"},{\"title\":\"Scallop: A Language for Neurosymbolic Programming\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2304.04812\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - scallop-lang/scallop: Framework and Language for Neurosymbolic Programming. · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/scallop-lang/scallop\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Scallop: A Language for Neurosymbolic Programming\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/369945806_Scallop_A_Language_for_Neurosymbolic_Programming\"},{\"title\":\"Neurosymbolic Programming in Scallop: Principles and Practice\",\"url\":\"https://www.cis.upenn.edu/~jianih/res/papers/scallop_principles_practice.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Scallop-lang.org\",\"url\":\"https://www.scallop-lang.org/\"},{\"title\":\"166 Scallop: A Language for Neurosymbolic Programming\",\"url\":\"https://www.cis.upenn.edu/~mhnaik/papers/pldi23.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Part 1: Reasoning over Implicit Graphs - Scallop\",\"url\":\"https://www.scallop-lang.org/log22/slides/log-2022-tutorial-scallop-part.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"NEUROSYMBOLIC PROGRAMMING IN SCALLOP: DESIGN, IMPLEMENTATION, AND APPLICATIONS\",\"url\":\"https://www.cis.upenn.edu/~mhnaik/theses/ziyang_li_thesis.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about Scallop and the related concepts in your query:\n\n## Scallop: A Neurosymbolic Programming Language\n\nScallop is a language that combines the benefits of deep learning and logical reasoning, enabling users to write a wide range of neurosymbolic applications and train them in a data- and compute-efficient manner.\n\n### Key Features\n\nScallop achieves its goals through three key features: 1) a flexible symbolic representation based on the relational data model; 2) a declarative logic programming language based on Datalog and supporting recursion, aggregation, and negation; and 3) a framework for automatic and efficient differentiable reasoning based on the theory of provenance semirings.\n\n### Core Capabilities\n\nScallop is a scalable Datalog solver equipped with support for discrete, probabilistic, and differentiable modes of reasoning, with these modes configurable to suit the needs of different AI applications.\n\n### Implementation and Integration\n\nScallop is relational and declarative, offering expressive reasoning capabilities such as recursion, negation, and aggregation, and supports discrete, probabilistic, and differentiable modes of reasoning for seamless integration with diverse neurosymbolic pipelines.\n\nThe search results don't specifically mention OR-Tools integration, though Scallop's architecture is designed for constraint programming and logical reasoning tasks common in that domain.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"5d353b7c-1a35-47ec-a570-e715d98e8ce3","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:00:21.895Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3d31a617-26bb-4149-a28c-a2be85b4871b","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"cb819447-840d-4e79-9d6c-b027dae69157","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6f37bbaf-3b2a-4889-9da8-014105b65b82","agentId":"a13cf5eff78c9773b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FVM56cRapLStr9EhQwPhVo","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"differentiable ILP inductive logic programming 2024 2025 scalability\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"A differentiable first-order rule learner for inductive logic programming - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0004370224000444\"},{\"title\":\"A differentiable first-order rule learner for inductive logic programming (Abstract Reprint) | IJCAI\",\"url\":\"https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2024/950\"},{\"title\":\"Published as a conference paper at ICLR 2025\",\"url\":\"https://proceedings.iclr.cc/paper_files/paper/2025/file/2f76a3a0f44263b5e56fec69ee1220f9-Paper-Conference.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"[2208.06652] Differentiable Inductive Logic Programming in High-Dimensional Space\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.06652\"},{\"title\":\"Inductive Logic Programming Essentials\",\"url\":\"https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/inductive-logic-programming-ilp\"},{\"title\":\"Differentiable Rule Induction from Raw Sequence Inputs | OpenReview\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/forum?id=zDjHOsSQxd\"},{\"title\":\"$$\\\\alpha$$ ILP: thinking visual scenes as differentiable logic programs | Machine Learning | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10994-023-06320-1\"},{\"title\":\"A differentiable first-order rule learner for inductive logic ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2024/0950.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"GLIDR: Graph-Like Inductive Logic Programming with Differentiable Reasoning\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2508.06716\"},{\"title\":\"Differentiable Inductive Logic Programming for Fraud Detection\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2410.21928v1\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here are the key findings on differentiable ILP and scalability in 2024-2025:\n\n## Recent Advances in Differentiable ILP\n\n### DFORL (2024)\nA novel differentiable ILP model called differentiable first-order rule learner (DFORL) is scalable to learn rules from both smaller and larger datasets. Experimental results indicate that DFORL is a precise, robust, scalable, and computationally cheap differentiable ILP model. A key advantage is that DFORL only needs the number of variables in the learned logic programs as input and does not need a strong language bias.\n\n### NeurRL (2025)\nNeurRL eliminates the candidate generation step required by ∂ILP and αILP, enhancing scalability. This work integrates a self-supervised differentiable clustering model with a novel differentiable ILP model, enabling rule learning from raw data without explicit label leakage.\n\n### GLIDR (2025)\nGLIDR (Graph-like Logical Induction with Differentiable Reasoning) is a differentiable ILP method that learns expressive graph-structured logical rules, and unlike existing differentiable ILP approaches limited to chain-like rule structures, GLIDR supports an expressive syntax that can represent rules with branches, cycles, and complex variable interactions. Experiments demonstrate GLIDR's expanded expressiveness enables it to significantly outperform existing rule learning methods while maintaining the noise robustness and scalability advantages of differentiable approaches.\n\n## Scalability Challenges and Solutions\n\nSymbolic ILP methods face robustness and scalability problems when learning from large-scale and ambiguous datasets. Matrix representations of logic programs are treated as trainable parameters in neural networks, enabling the model to support learning from both small and large datasets in a CPU computation device within an acceptable running time.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"657bf5f2-db27-4135-b199-0cbd39801e80","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:00:22.834Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"cb819447-840d-4e79-9d6c-b027dae69157","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"aaecc3d6-675a-44a1-bc5c-3bbad1a34fa0","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6f37bbaf-3b2a-4889-9da8-014105b65b82","agentId":"a13cf5eff78c9773b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015tnKrneD2KAyveNU9rkFZj","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"neuro-symbolic CSP constraint satisfaction sound guarantee 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"[2503.19466] A Probabilistic Neuro-symbolic Layer for Algebraic Constraint Satisfaction\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.19466\"},{\"title\":\"ConstraintLLM: A Neuro-Symbolic Framework for Industrial ...\",\"url\":\"https://aclanthology.org/2025.emnlp-main.809.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"A Probabilistic Neuro-symbolic Layer for Algebraic Constraint Satisfaction | OpenReview\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/forum?id=OXaKsbjyL3\"},{\"title\":\"A Probabilistic Neuro-symbolic Layer for Algebraic Constraint Satisfaction\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2503.19466\"},{\"title\":\"Machine Learning Algorithms for Assisting Solvers for ...\",\"url\":\"https://optimization-online.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/ML_CSP.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"ConstraintLLM: A Neuro-Symbolic Framework for Industrial-Level Constraint Programming | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/397422662_ConstraintLLM_A_Neuro-Symbolic_Framework_for_Industrial-Level_Constraint_Programming\"},{\"title\":\"A Probabilistic Neuro-symbolic Layer for Algebraic Constraint ...\",\"url\":\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mlresearch/v286/main/assets/kurscheidt25a/kurscheidt25a.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Google Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=A+Probabilistic+Neuro-symbolic+Layer+for+Algebraic+Constraint+Satisfaction.\"},{\"title\":\"NeuS 2025\",\"url\":\"https://neus-2025.github.io/\"},{\"title\":\"MIT Open Access Articles Solving Constraint-Satisfaction Problems with\",\"url\":\"https://dspace.mit.edu/bitstream/handle/1721.1/120124/neco_a_01074.pdf?sequence=1&isAllowed=y\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here are the key findings regarding neuro-symbolic approaches to constraint satisfaction with sound guarantees in 2025:\n\n## Probabilistic Neuro-Symbolic Layer for Algebraic Constraint Satisfaction\n\nThe most relevant work is \"A Probabilistic Neuro-symbolic Layer for Algebraic Constraint Satisfaction,\" which introduces a differentiable probabilistic layer that guarantees the satisfaction of non-convex algebraic constraints over continuous variables and can be seamlessly plugged into any neural architecture and trained via maximum likelihood without requiring approximations.\n\n### Technical Approach\n\nThis probabilistic algebraic layer (PAL) defines a distribution over conjunctions and disjunctions of linear inequalities, parameterized by polynomials, enabling efficient and exact renormalization via symbolic integration, which can be amortized across different data points and easily parallelized on a GPU.\n\n### Key Motivation\n\nWhile simple approaches encourage constraint satisfaction via loss penalties at training time, such methods don't guarantee that invalid configurations will be associated with exactly zero probability, which could have catastrophic consequences at test time in safety-critical applications where even small probabilities of constraint violation can be harmful.\n\n## ConstraintLLM Framework\n\nAnother 2025 approach is ConstraintLLM: A Neuro-Symbolic Framework for Industrial-Level Constraint Programming, though this focuses more on using language models for CSP/COP solving rather than providing formal safety guarantees.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"25b170de-10f9-42bd-8260-6c947c736e47","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:00:23.830Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"aaecc3d6-675a-44a1-bc5c-3bbad1a34fa0","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
